            <ArticleTitle>The effect of watching funny and unfunny videos on post-surgical pain levels.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>36-41</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1744-3881(16)30154-2</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ctcp.2016.11.003</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The aim of this study was to describe pain intensity before and after watching funny and unfunny videos on post-surgical pain levels.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Ninety patients were divided into three groups. Group A watched funny videos, Group B watched unfunny videos, Group C did not watch any videos. Pain assessment of the patients was carried out before (first), immediately after (second), and 30 min after (third) watching videos.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The first pain scoremean in Group A was 40.83 ± 22.62, in Group B was 37.46 ± 24.31, in Group C was 48.86 ± 24.06. The second pain scoremean in Group A was 32.93 ± 20.28, in Group B was 29.76 ± 20.26, in Group C was 48.96 ± 24.11. The third pain scoremean in Group A was 37.43 ± 23.07, in Group B was 36.80 ± 22.67, in Group C was 52.30 ± 23.97.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Watching funny or unfunny videos reduces the post-surgical pain level.</AbstractText>
